.Dd November 14, 2026
.Dt PRCTL 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm prctl ,
.Nm personality
.Nd operations on the calling process
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/prctl.h
.Ft int
.Fn prctl "int option" "..."
.In sys/personality.h
.Ft int
.Fn personality "unsigned long persona"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn prctl
reads or changes an attribute of the calling process, named by
.Fa option .
The arguments after it are unsigned longs, the second through the fifth.
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width "PR_SET_CHILD_SUBREAPER"
.It Dv PR_SET_PDEATHSIG No (1)
Set the signal the caller receives when its parent exits, from 1 to 64,
or 0 for none.
The signal is posted when the parent exits, after the caller has been
given to its new parent, so a handler that asks for its parent finds
that one.
.It Dv PR_GET_PDEATHSIG No (2)
Store that signal as an int at the second argument.
.It Dv PR_GET_DUMPABLE No (3)
Return whether the caller is dumpable, 1 or 0.
.It Dv PR_SET_DUMPABLE No (4)
Set it to the second argument, 0 or 1.
The flag belongs to the address space, as on Linux: the threads of a
process, and a child that
.Xr clone 2
made with
.Dv CLONE_VM ,
share one, so what one of them sets the others read.
.It Dv PR_SET_NAME No (15)
Set the caller's program name, which
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /stat
and
.Xr ps 1
show, to the string at the second argument, cut to 15 bytes.
.It Dv PR_GET_NAME No (16)
Store the program name at the second argument: 16 bytes, padded with
null bytes.
.It Dv PR_GET_SECCOMP No (21)
Return the thread's seccomp mode: 0 for none, 1 strict, 2 filter.
.It Dv PR_SET_SECCOMP No (22)
Set the strict mode for a second argument of 1, or add the filter at the
third argument for 2, as
.Xr seccomp 2
does.
.It Dv PR_CAPBSET_READ No (23)
Return 1 when the capability the second argument names, 0 to 40, is in the
caller's bounding set, and 0 when it is not; see
.Xr capget 2 .
.It Dv PR_CAPBSET_DROP No (24)
Take the capability the second argument names out of the caller's
bounding set, for good: no exec gives it again.
The caller needs
.Dv CAP_SETPCAP .
A thread that holds the capability keeps it.
.It Dv PR_SET_TIMERSLACK No (29)
Set the calling thread's timer slack, how many nanoseconds past the time
it asks for a
.Xr nanosleep 2
or
.Fn clock_nanosleep
of the thread lasts, to the second argument, or for 0 to the thread's
default, the slack it had when it was created.
The slack starts at 50000.
A thread under a real-time or deadline policy has none, and the call
changes nothing for it; a change of policy to one of those sets the slack
to 0, and a change back gives the default back.
.It Dv PR_GET_TIMERSLACK No (30)
Return the calling thread's timer slack in nanoseconds.
.It Dv PR_SET_CHILD_SUBREAPER No (36)
Make the caller's process a child subreaper when the second argument is
not 0, and clear the mark when it is 0.
When a process ends, each of its children goes to the nearest of its
ancestors, going up by parent, that is a child subreaper and has a thread
that has not ended.
The search stops at the first init it meets, the system's or that of a
PID namespace, the ending process included, and with no subreaper the
child goes to init, or to the init of its PID namespace, as it does
otherwise.
A child that has already ended is announced to its new parent with
.Dv SIGCHLD ,
and the new parent reaps it with
.Xr wait 2
and counts its CPU time as that of any child it reaps.
.Pa /proc/PID/task/PID/children
lists a process's children; see
.Xr proc 5 .
.It Dv PR_GET_CHILD_SUBREAPER No (37)
Store the mark, 0 or 1, as an int at the second argument.
.It Dv PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS No (38)
With the second argument 1 and the rest 0, set the caller's no_new_privs
bit.
It cannot be cleared.
.It Dv PR_GET_NO_NEW_PRIVS No (39)
With every other argument 0, return the no_new_privs bit.
.It Dv PR_SET_THP_DISABLE No (41)
With the last three arguments 0, turn transparent huge pages off in the
caller's address space when the second argument is not 0, and back on
when it is: no fault, khugepaged or
.Dv MADV_COLLAPSE
gives it a huge page meanwhile, and the huge pages already there stay.
A fork's child and an exec's new image keep the setting.
.It Dv PR_GET_THP_DISABLE No (42)
With every other argument 0, return the setting, 0 or 1.
.It Dv PR_CAP_AMBIENT No (47)
The ambient set, the capabilities an exec keeps for the new image
.Pq see Xr capget 2 .
The second argument is the operation and the third a capability of 0 to
40; the fourth and fifth must be 0.
.Dv PR_CAP_AMBIENT_IS_SET
(1) returns 1 when the capability is in the set and 0 when not.
.Dv PR_CAP_AMBIENT_RAISE
(2) adds it, when the thread holds it and has it in its inheritable set.
.Dv PR_CAP_AMBIENT_LOWER
(3) takes it out.
.Dv PR_CAP_AMBIENT_CLEAR_ALL
(4), with the third argument 0 too, empties the set.
bubblewrap raises each capability its
.Fl -cap-add
keeps, so that the command it runs holds it.
.El
.Pp
The attributes follow the process as on Linux.
.Xr fork 2
passes the dumpable flag, the no_new_privs bit and the timer slack, which
becomes the child's default, to the child and not the parent-death signal
or the child subreaper mark.
The mark belongs to the process, so every thread of it reads and sets the
one mark, and
.Xr execve 2
keeps it.
.Xr execve 2
makes the process dumpable again, or not dumpable when the new program
runs with an effective ID that differs from its real one or when the
caller may not read the program or its interpreter.
An exec with such an effective ID also clears the parent-death signal,
and so does a change of the effective user or group ID by an exec or by
.Xr setuid 2
and its relatives, which clears the dumpable flag as well.
Under no_new_privs,
.Xr execve 2
ignores the set-user-ID and set-group-ID bits of the program, as it
ignores them on a nosuid mount.
.Pp
The dumpable flag decides whether a tracer without effective user ID 0
may attach to the process, as
.Xr ptrace 2
describes, and whether the process may leave a core file, as
.Xr core 5
describes.
.Ss personality
.Fn personality ,
number 136 on i386 and 135 on x86-64, sets the caller's execution domain to
.Fa persona
and returns the old value, or with
.Fa persona
0xFFFFFFFF returns it alone.
The domain, the low byte, must be
.Dv PER_LINUX
(0) or
.Dv PER_LINUX32
(8), else the call fails with
.Er EINVAL .
The flag bits above it are kept.
On the x86-64 kernel
.Dv PER_LINUX32
makes
.Xr uname 2
report the machine as
.Ql i686 .
.Dv ADDR_NO_RANDOMIZE
(0x0040000), which GDB and
.Ql setarch -R
set, makes the next
.Xr execve 2
place the new image at fixed addresses on x86-64, where they are otherwise
randomized; the other flags change nothing.
A child of
.Xr fork 2
inherits the value, and
.Xr execve 2
keeps it, except for an image that runs with IDs its caller lacks, or
with capabilities given back, which loses
.Dv ADDR_NO_RANDOMIZE ,
.Dv READ_IMPLIES_EXEC ,
.Dv ADDR_COMPAT_LAYOUT
and
.Dv MMAP_PAGE_ZERO ,
as on Linux.
.Ss System call interface
The i386 system call number is 172, and the x86-64 one 157, where the five
arguments are in RDI, RSI, RDX, R10 and R8.
EBX contains
.Fa option
and ECX through EDI the second through fifth arguments.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Dv PR_GET_DUMPABLE ,
.Dv PR_CAPBSET_READ ,
.Dv PR_GET_SECCOMP
and
.Dv PR_GET_NO_NEW_PRIVS
return the value asked for; the other options return 0.
On failure
.Fn prctl
returns -1 and sets
.Va errno .
The kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/musl/prctlmusl.c
sets and reads each attribute, checks that a child receives its
parent-death signal when its parent exits, that a fork passes on what it
should, that a change of the effective user ID clears the dumpable flag
and the signal, and that a program with the set-user-ID bit runs without
its owner's ID under no_new_privs.
It also makes a child subreaper and checks that the orphans of its
grandchild come to it, the one that had ended and the one that had not,
that its wait reaps them and counts the CPU time of the second, and that
its children file lists each child while it is one; and that with the
mark cleared they go to init instead.
.Pa tests/musl/sandboxmusl.c
reads and drops capabilities of the bounding set, and
.Pa tests/musl/capmusl.c
raises, lowers and clears ambient capabilities and checks that an exec
keeps them.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EFAULT]"
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The name, the signal or the child subreaper mark cannot be read or
written at the second argument.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa option
is none of those above, the signal is past 64, the dumpable value is
neither 0 nor 1, or the arguments of the no_new_privs options are not as
above, or a capability is past 40, or the ambient operation is none of the
four or has an argument that must be 0 and is not.
Every option Linux has and this kernel does not is refused this way, as
Linux refuses an option it does not know.
.It Bq Er EPERM
.Dv PR_CAPBSET_DROP
by a thread without
.Dv CAP_SETPCAP ,
or
.Dv PR_CAP_AMBIENT_RAISE
of a capability the thread does not hold or that is not in its
inheritable set.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr capget 2 ,
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr nanosleep 2 ,
.Xr seccomp 2 ,
.Xr setuid 2 ,
.Xr sigaction 2 ,
.Xr wait 2 ,
.Xr core 5 ,
.Xr proc 5
